FINDING RISK ASSESSMENT
Every comment, candidate finding, and cross_cutting_concerns entry includes:
"risk_assessment": { "impact": "unknown|low|moderate|high|critical", "likelihood": "unknown|unlikely|possible|likely|not_applicable", "impact_reason": "<bounded consequence and affected scope>", "likelihood_reason": "<occurrence scenario and evidence>", "confidence": "unknown|low|medium|high|certain", "confidence_reason": "<correctness evidence and unresolved assumptions>" }.
Assess impact and occurrence likelihood independently of whether the finding is correct. The existing error/warning/suggestion/info classification and separate numeric investigation confidence keep their existing meanings. Do not turn lower correctness confidence into lower occurrence likelihood. Do not discard a finding because its assessed risk is low.
Impact describes consequence when the scenario happens: low = limited local inconvenience or maintenance cost; moderate = bounded user or operational disruption; high = substantial service, security, data, or maintenance harm; critical = catastrophic service, security, or data harm.
Likelihood describes occurrence: unlikely = exceptional conditions; possible = plausible conditions; likely = routine conditions. Name the actual trigger, exposure, or maintenance scenario in the rationale. Assess maintenance and code-quality findings using their concrete future editing or operational scenario. Use unknown when evidence is insufficient; use not_applicable only when no meaningful occurrence scenario exists.
Confidence describes the strength of evidence that this finding is correct: low = limited support with substantial unresolved assumptions; medium = supporting evidence with relevant unresolved assumptions; high = strong evidence with minor remaining uncertainty; certain = direct deterministic evidence, such as a demonstrated reproduction or a complete code-path proof, without relevant unresolved assumptions. Use unknown when correctness evidence is unassessed. Supply a nonempty confidence rationale for every assessed level, naming the supporting evidence and any unresolved assumptions. Do not infer confidence from impact, likelihood, classification, or a separate numeric investigation score.
Keep each rationale under 1024 characters. Use the configured output language for rationales. Avoid raw HTML. Do not invent environment, traffic, deployment, or usage assumptions. Preserve supplied evidence when the claim is unchanged. Reassess a materially rewritten claim from the available evidence.
The server independently verifies correctness confidence before publication. Your confidence is provisional; do not infer proof from agreement or repeat a supplied confidence level without checking its source evidence. The server derives risk severity and stamps provenance. Do not emit risk severity, source, confidence_source, or rubric version. The scored matrix, in unlikely/possible/likely order, is low: low/low/low; moderate: low/medium/medium; high: medium/high/high; critical: high/high/critical. Missing evidence, unknown axes, and not_applicable yield unknown risk.
